 in  returning to their jobs and so  that's something that is it continues to  be a challenge because we know that  demand is increasing in las vegas  and uh it's extremely exciting we've got  world of concrete as you said coming  back and so  um the the increased demand is just  wonderful to see  and as long as we can get people to come  back to work uh  you know i know i was talking with uh  the golden nugget  the other day and they're looking for  housekeepers to come back to work and so  they're looking for ways to  attract those individuals to come back  to their jobs and we're also looking at  upskilling and reskilling some of these  individuals so that  so that they can be more prepared for  other opportunities as well  just to finish on this discussion is  there a concern on your part of  sort of las vegas getting ahead of its  skis a little bit when it comes to  supply and demand there's so much demand  but if there isn't that supply of  workforce that vegas might  the valley might get ahead of itself a  little bit well i i think  you know there are always concerns we  have to be very smart  and um you know we have to really make  sure that we  are prepared and in some cases you'll  see  longer lines and things like that so so  there are challenges with the increased  demand  but i think overall um employers across  the state  and especially in southern nevada are  doing an excellent job of  being being very creative about how  we're handling that increased demand  the legislative session just wrapped up  up in carson city it was a little bit of  a challenge to be there in person i know  but  let's talk about some of the major bills  quickly that have been  passed by the legislature on the  governor's desk  and the impact on local business well it  was this was one of the most daunting  legislative sessions i've  personally ever seen it was there were a  lot of proposals that if they would have  passed would have significantly harmed  employers and small bu